findings.body:
The PR description says to use `zmalloc_size`, but the code calls `malloc_usable_size` directly:

::code{file="src/object.c" lines="1213-1214"}

In `zmalloc.h`, `zmalloc_size` maps to `je_malloc_usable_size` under jemalloc. It maps to `malloc_size` on macOS, and to `malloc_usable_size` only on glibc, FreeBSD and similar. Redis builds jemalloc with a `je_` prefix, so a bare `malloc_usable_size` either resolves to libc's function or doesn't exist. With jemalloc, which is the default on Linux, libc would be asked about a jemalloc pointer, giving undefined behaviour or a wrong size. On macOS the call doesn't compile because `<malloc.h>` isn't included. The code is also inconsistent with the other branches, which all use `zmalloc_size`.

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1214"}
    size_t asize = zmalloc_size((void *)o);

The old code divided the sampled bytes by `elecount` (entries in the sampled nodes) and multiplied by `ql->count` (total entries). That gives a per-entry average times the number of entries. The PR divides by `samples` (number of nodes sampled) but still multiplies by `ql->count`. In `quicklist.h`, `count` is the total number of entries and `len` is the number of nodes. The result is the average node size times the number of entries, which overestimates by roughly a factor of entries-per-node. A list of 100k small entries could report hundreds of times its real size. `elecount` is now also set but unused, which will trigger a compiler warning. This change is unrelated to the key-embedding fix.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1230-1241"}

Either revert to `elesize/elecount*ql->count`, or use `elesize/samples*ql->len`. Note also that the `do {} while` loop dereferences `ql->head` without a NULL check.
